    <h2>Signs your pet’s mouth hurts</h2>
    <p>Pets very rarely stop eating because of a sore mouth. Watch instead for:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Bad breath that is more than “dog breath”.</li>
        <li>Chewing on one side, dropping food, or swallowing kibble whole.</li>
        <li>Pawing at the face, or not wanting it touched.</li>
        <li>Red gums, brown tartar, or a broken tooth.</li>
        <li>A cat who suddenly prefers soft food.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>What happens on dental day</h2>
    <p>Your pet comes in first thing and gets a check and a calming pre-medication. Under anaesthetic, with a nurse watching heart, breathing, oxygen and temperature throughout, we chart every tooth, take full-mouth X-rays, clean above and below the gum line and polish the surface smooth.</p>
    <p>If a tooth is badly diseased we call you before taking it out. Extractions are done with nerve blocks so your pet wakes up comfortable, and they go home that afternoon with pain relief and soft food instructions.</p>
    <p class="callout">Owners are always surprised how much brighter their pet seems a week after a dental. That is what living without a toothache looks like.</p>
